  "narrative": "The newly analyzed broad predictors are mostly below threshold or directionally unsupported. ERBB2 amplification does not support the claimed stronger-dependency direction either overall (signed +0.018, interval -0.094 to +0.131) or after excluding TP53/SMARCA4/KEAP1/PTEN losses (signed -0.009, interval -0.081 to +0.062). BRCA2 loss is also unsupported overall (signed -0.007) and in the central-loss-intact population (signed -0.061). NF1 loss is null for the claimed weaker-dependency direction overall (signed -0.002) and in the central-loss-intact population (signed -0.015). MTAP loss with CDKN2A intact in high-quality screens gives a small positive signed estimate of only +0.046, with an interval including zero and only marginally reaching the threshold; given earlier MTAP null results, this is not meaningful. Media serum 10% versus 5% is essentially null (signed +0.002), and Avana versus non-Avana library (signed -0.052) and matched RNA/DNA availability versus unmatched availability (signed +0.030) are also not threshold-level offsets. I therefore reject and close these hypotheses as not supporting scientifically meaningful associations in the claimed direction. These technical nulls are useful: they reduce concern that the central biological associations are simply platform, serum, or omics-availability artifacts, although residual confounding cannot be excluded.\n\nThe major new signal is MSI-high, but it is opposite the registered direction. H43 and H105 hypothesized that MSI-high is associated with higher composite scores, i.e. weaker dependency. Instead, the signed estimates are large and negative: -0.792 overall (interval -0.907 to -0.676) and -0.754 in the central-loss-intact population (interval -0.825 to -0.683). Because these are positive-direction claims, the negative signed estimates indicate that MSI-high cells have much lower, more negative dependency_composite values, i.e. stronger composite dependency. The effect is far beyond the 0.15-unit threshold in the opposite direction and remains strong when TP53, SMARCA4, KEAP1, and PTEN losses are excluded. I therefore reject H43 and H105 as written and close those investigations, but I register new reversed claims testing MSI-high as a predictor of stronger dependency: overall, central-loss-intact, and adenocarcinoma-restricted. I leave those proposals unresolved because the reversal is data-driven and should receive its own direct evidence signed to the reverse direction before any acceptance or validation decision.\n\nGiven the large observed reverse MSI-high signal, I do not request validation now. Validating the original positive-direction H43/H105 would test the same contrast under an inconvenient sign convention and would not by itself produce clean evidence for the reversed claim. The next analysis should first create direct reverse-direction evidence. I defer lower-priority untested subtype, library, and omics-availability flags, as well as untested MTAP main effects, because available evidence is strongly below threshold or technically null. The most useful next focus is to test the reversed MSI-high claims and, if direct signed evidence supports them, decide whether validation or context refinement is more informative.",
  "research_notes": "Iteration 6 appraise update: Broad secondary predictor screening is complete. ERBB2 amplification, BRCA2 loss, NF1 loss, MTAP CDKN2A-intact high-quality screen, media serum 10 vs 5, Avana vs non-Avana, and matched RNA/DNA availability all fail threshold-level support and are closed as unsupported in claimed directions. This makes technical assay/culture offsets less likely to explain the central associations, though confounding remains possible.\n\nKey new data-driven finding: MSI-high is strongly opposite to the originally registered positive direction. H43: signed -0.792, interval -0.907 to -0.676. H105: signed -0.754, interval -0.825 to -0.683 after excluding TP53/SMARCA4/KEAP1/PTEN losses. Interpretation in dependency_composite terms: MSI-high cells have much more negative composite scores, indicating stronger dependency. The effect is huge and robust to central-loss exclusion, but the reversal was discovered post hoc, so the original positive-direction claims are rejected/closed and new reversed claims are registered for direct signed evidence: main, central-loss-intact, and adenocarcinoma.\n\nNo validation requested yet because validation of original H43/H105 would keep the positive-direction sign convention and would not directly support acceptance of the reversed claim. Next analyze stage should run the new reversed MSI-high proposals. If reverse evidence remains large and precise, then either validation or subtype/central/interaction refinement can be considered. Keep all conclusions as supplied-dataset associations, not causal claims or generalizable claims.\n\nCurrent central accepted landscape remains KEAP1 loss and TP53 loss associated with higher/less negative scores (weaker dependency); SMARCA4 loss and PTEN loss associated with lower/more negative scores (stronger dependency). CDKN2A is weakly suggestive but not threshold-robust and closed as low priority. MTAP is not independently supported."
}

